  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 320 AM Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Unsettled weather and above-normal rain chances are \nexpected through Sat evening, with isolated heavy downpours \npossible. A few strong storms also possible on Fri.\n\nWe will stay in a moist and unsettled pattern through late Sat, as \nbaggy mid level troughing holds from the E Great Lakes/SW Quebec \ndown through W NC/AL/GA, sandwiched between a strong anticyclone \ncentered over AZ/NM and another anticyclone centered offshore SE of \nBermuda. Yesterday's below-normal PW (~0.75-1.1\") has already been \nsupplanted by values of 1.5-2.0\" as a deep wet flow through the \ncolumn continues on the E side of the mid level trough. These \nanomalous PWs (110-130% of normal, highest along/E of Hwy 1) \ncombined with periodic upper level speed maxes rotating through the \ntrough base to the E side of the trough (yielding modest DPVA) and \nthe passing bouts of RRQ upper divergence will act on this moisture \nto squeeze out scattered showers across our area, with a few mainly \nafternoon and evening thunderstorms possible. Dewpoints returning to \nthe low-mid 70s and partial heating through a mix of sun and clouds \nwill help push daily SBCAPE to peaks of 1500-2500 J/kg each day \nthrough Sat, highest mid-late afternoon, although the deep layer \nbulk shear stays quite borderline at around 20 kts and mid level \nlapse rates are likely to hold at 5.3-5.9 C/km, which should limit \nstorm organization and strength respectively. But the elevated PW \nvalues especially along/E of Hwy 1 along with expectations of a deep \nwarm cloud layer over 4 km favoring warm rain processes and the \npotential for slow storm motion and training will support pockets of \nheavy downpours and high localize rain totals. The expected limited \ncoverage of heavy rain clusters precludes consideration of a flood \nwatch, but at least a few flood advisories may be needed. Rain \nchances will head back down late in the weekend as the upper level \nshear axis pivots clockwise to a W-E orientation and pushes to our \nS, allowing mid-upper ridging to build from the mid Miss Valley and \nlower Ohio Valley through the Mid Atlantic. \n\nRegarding the potential for strong storms, a few wet microbursts \nwill be possible each afternoon/evening, with the highest risk for \nstrong storms on Fri, when SPC has us in an area of Marginal Risk \n(level 1 of 5) for severe storms. Again, the bulk shear won't be \nespecially strong, but the moderate CAPE and possible dynamic \ncontributions from upstream MCVs and RRQ upper divergence could tip \nthe scales to more mature and long-lived storms on Fri. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Above-normal temperatures likely to end August and \npersist into early September.\n\nThe persistent anomalous mid/upper lvl ridge and anticyclone over \nthe Four Corners region will be displaced by a series of shortwave \ntroughs progressing through the PacNW and northern/central \nIntermountain West. This process will gradually build mid/upper lvl \nheights over the Deep South and TN Valley early next week and likely \npersisting into early September. \n\nThis height pattern will be favorable for positive H850 temp \nanomalies to spread into the region and favor well-above normal \ntemperatures. Although the temperature forecast will naturally be \ncomplicated by late morning and afternoon convective chances, deep \nlayer of anomalous lower/mid tropospheric temps, and close proximity \nwith the deep subtropical ridge may help mitigate the widespread \nconvective chances. As a result, temperatures in the mid/upper 90s \nwill be possible Tues onward with the experimental HeatRisk having a \n40-70% chance of reaching the Major risk category.\n\nThe European model suite and related AI guidance holds this pattern \nwell into the first full week of September while the GFS model suite \nshows the potential to break down the ridge and retrograde it \nwestward as early as late next week.",
